    "https://upike.bamboohr.com/careers/529": {
      "title": "WS - Medical Humanities Research Assistant - FWS",
      "description": "WS - Medical Humanities Research Assistant - FWS. Department: FWS - Student Employment. Student Worker. Medical Humanities Research Assistant Division: CAS Department: Humanities Experience: Entry-Level Supervisor of Student Employee(s): Robert Musick FWS, IWS, or Grant Funded: ☒ FWS, ☐ IWS, ☐ Grant Funded Avg. Hours/Week: ☐ 1-5, ☒ 6-10, ☐ 11-15, ☐ 16-20 Requested Start Date: 08/25/2026 Term of Position: ☐ Summer, ☒ Fall, ☒ Spring The Medical Humanities Research Assistant is a Federal Work-Study position that supports faculty and staff in conducting interdisciplinary research at the intersection of medicine, healthcare, ethics, humanities, and community health. The student employee assists with literature reviews, data collection, research organization, and scholarly dissemination while developing research, analytical, and professional skills in an academic environment. Responsibilities and Career Competencies Conduct literature reviews and summarize relevant healthcare and medical humanities research. (CT, T, CSD) Collect, organize, and manage research data while maintaining confidentiality and ethical standards. (CT, P, T) Prepare research summaries, bibliographies, briefs, presentations, posters, manuscripts, and grant materials. (C, CT, T, CSD) Participate in research meetings and collaborate with faculty, staff, students, and community partners. (C, TW, P) Support community-engaged research, including outreach, surveys, interviews, and educational activities. (C, TW, CTR) Use research software and digital tools to maintain data, files, and project documentation. (T, CT, P) Analyze findings, identify trends, and contribute to research reports. (CT, C) Manage assignments and deadlines independently while demonstrating initiative and professional growth. (CSD, L, P) Competencies/Skills: Communication (C), Teamwork (TW), Professionalism (P), Career & Self Development (CSD), Critical Thinking (CT), Community Transformation (CTR), Leadership (L), Technology (T) Qualifications Current UPIKE student in good academic standing. Strong written, communication, organizational, and critical-thinking skills. Ability to conduct academic research and use databases, Microsoft Office/Google Workspace, and related technology. Ability to maintain confidentiality and follow ethical research practices. Dependable, detail-oriented, and able to work independently and collaboratively. Interest in medical humanities, healthcare, public health, ethics, or related research preferred. In the following sections check all boxes that apply to your job. Computer Skills: ☒ Word, ☒ Excel, ☒ PowerPoint, ☐ Canva, ☐ Social Media & New Technology, ☐ Other: _________________________ Other Skills: ☒ Attention to detail, ☒ Strong active listening abilities, ☐ Public speaking, ☒ Written & verbal communication, ☐ Customer focused, ☒ Willingness to learn new things Work Schedule: ☐ Su ___ to ___; ☐ M ___ to ___; ☐ T ___ to ___; ☐ W ___ to ___; ☐ R ___ to ___; ☐ F ___ to ___; ☐ Sa ___ to ___; ☒ Hours vary depending on workload (Check days that apply, insert hours if set, otherwise, check “Hours vary depending on workload”.) Work Conditions: ☐ Heat, ☐ Cold, ☐ Odors/Smoke, ☐ Toxic Agents, ☐ Dust, ☐ Machinery, ☐ Light Intensity, ☐ Noise, ☐ Vibrations, ☐ Wetness, ☐ Humidity, ☐ Electrical Currents, ☐ Other: _________________________ Physical Demands: ☒ Keyboarding, ☐ Reaching/Handling, ☐ Stooping/Kneeling, ☐ Crouching/Crawling, ☐ Pushing up to ___ lbs., ☐ Pulling up to ___ lbs., ☐ Lifting up to ___ lbs., ☐ Carrying up to ___ lbs., ☐ Climbing, ☐ Balancing, ☐ Other: _________________________ Number of Positions: One Pay Rate: 8.75/Hour Location: Building: ADM, Room #: 000 Application Notes: Resumes and applications will be reviewed to determine if you meet the required qualifications for the position. If it is determined that you meet the required qualifications, your application materials will be used to identify a top group of the most highly qualified candidates. The University of Pikeville is an equal opportunity employer committed to assembling a diverse, broadly trained faculty and staff. The University of Pikeville does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, age or disabilities in its programs, activities, hiring, or the admission of students."
    },
    "https://upike.bamboohr.com/careers/530": {
      "title": "WS - Suicide Prevention and Awareness Research Assistant - FWS",
      "description": "WS - Suicide Prevention and Awareness Research Assistant - FWS. Department: FWS - Student Employment. Student Worker. Suicide Prevention and Awareness Research Assistant Division: Student Affair Department: Health & Wellness Experience: Entry-Level Supervisor of Student Employee(s): Robert Musick and Glenna Henson FWS, IWS, or Grant Funded: ☒ FWS, ☐ IWS, ☐ Grant Funded Avg. Hours/Week: ☐ 1-5, ☒ 6-10, ☐ 11-15, ☐ 16-20 Requested Start Date: 08/25/2026 Term of Position: ☐ Summer, ☒ Fall, ☒ Spring The Suicide Prevention and Awareness Research Assistant is a Federal Work-Study position that supports faculty and staff in conducting interdisciplinary research at the intersection of medicine, sociology, ethics, humanities, and psychology, and community field research. The student employee assists with literature reviews, data collection, research organization, and scholarly dissemination while developing research, analytical, and professional skills in an academic environment. Responsibilities and Career Competencies Conduct literature reviews using academic databases to identify relevant research in suicidology, public health, sociology, medical humanities, or related disciplines. CT, T, CSD Assist with collecting, organizing, and managing research data while maintaining accuracy, confidentiality, and ethical research practices. CT, P, T Prepare summaries, annotated bibliographies, and research briefs for faculty investigators and project teams. C, CT, T Assist with the preparation of presentations, posters, manuscripts, grant proposals, and conference materials. C, CT, T, CSD Participate in research meetings by contributing ideas, taking meeting notes, and collaborating with faculty, staff, and student researchers. TW, C, P Support community-engaged research projects by assisting with outreach activities, surveys, interviews, or educational events when appropriate. CTR, C, TW Utilize research software, spreadsheets, and digital collaboration tools to organize project materials and maintain research documentation. T, CT Maintain organized electronic and physical research files while following institutional policies regarding confidentiality and responsible conduct of research. P, T Assist with evaluating research findings by identifying trends, organizing data, and contributing to project reports. CT, C Demonstrate initiative by completing assigned tasks independently, managing deadlines, and pursuing opportunities for professional growth in research and suicidology scholarship. CSD, L, P Competencies/Skills Developed Communication (C): Develop professional written and verbal communication through research writing, presentations, and collaboration. Teamwork (TW): Collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, students, and community partners on interdisciplinary research projects. Professionalism (P): Demonstrate ethical conduct, accountability, confidentiality, and responsible research practices. Career & Self Development (CSD): Build research experience, strengthen academic and career readiness, and pursue continuous learning. Critical Thinking (CT): Analyze scholarly literature, evaluate evidence, solve research problems, and synthesize information. Community Transformation (CTR): Contribute to research that improves healthcare, community well-being, and health equity through community-engaged scholarship. Leadership (L): Show initiative, assume responsibility for assigned research activities, and contribute to collaborative project success. Technology (T): Utilize academic databases, reference management software, spreadsheets, research platforms, and digital collaboration tools. Qualifications: Add qualifications needed to be successful in this position. In the following sections check all boxes that apply to your job. Computer Skills: ☒ Word, ☒ Excel, ☒ PowerPoint, ☐ Canva, ☐ Social Media & New Technology, ☐ Other: _________________________ Other Skills: ☒ Attention to detail, ☒ Strong active listening abilities, ☐ Public speaking, ☒ Written & verbal communication, ☐ Customer focused, ☒ Willingness to learn new things Work Schedule: ☐ Su ___ to ___; ☐ M ___ to ___; ☐ T ___ to ___; ☐ W ___ to ___; ☐ R ___ to ___; ☐ F ___ to ___; ☐ Sa ___ to ___; ☒ Hours vary depending on workload (Check days that apply, insert hours if set, otherwise, check “Hours vary depending on workload”.) Work Conditions: ☐ Heat, ☐ Cold, ☐ Odors/Smoke, ☐ Toxic Agents, ☐ Dust, ☐ Machinery, ☐ Light Intensity, ☐ Noise, ☐ Vibrations, ☐ Wetness, ☐ Humidity, ☐ Electrical Currents, ☐ Other: _________________________ Physical Demands: ☒ Keyboarding, ☐ Reaching/Handling, ☐ Stooping/Kneeling, ☐ Crouching/Crawling, ☐ Pushing up to ___ lbs., ☐ Pulling up to ___ lbs., ☐ Lifting up to ___ lbs., ☐ Carrying up to ___ lbs., ☐ Climbing, ☐ Balancing, ☐ Other: _________________________ Number of Positions: One Pay Rate: 8.75/Hour Location: Building: Record, Room #: 600 Application Notes: Resumes and applications will be reviewed to determine if you meet the required qualifications for the position. If it is determined that you meet the required qualifications, your application materials will be used to identify a top group of the most highly qualified candidates. The University of Pikeville is an equal opportunity employer committed to assembling a diverse, broadly trained faculty and staff. The University of Pikeville does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, sex, gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, age or disabilities in its programs, activities, hiring, or the admission of students."
    },
    "https://upike.bamboohr.com/careers/531": {
      "title": "WS - Spiritual Life Assistant - IWS",
      "description": "WS - Spiritual Life Assistant - IWS. Department: FWS - Student Employment. Student Worker. Spiritual Life Assistant Division: Student Affairs Department: Office of Chaplain Experience: Entry-Level Supervisor of Student Employee(s): Robert Musick FWS, IWS, or Grant Funded: ☐ FWS, ☒ IWS, ☐ Grant Funded Avg. Hours/Week: ☐ 1-5, ☒ 6-10, ☐ 11-15, ☐ 16-20 Requested Start Date: 08/25/2026 Term of Position: ☐ Summer, ☒ Fall, ☒ Spring The Spiritual Life Assistant is a position that supports the Office of Spiritual Life by assisting with campus programs, worship services, community service initiatives, and student engagement activities that foster spiritual growth and holistic well-being. The student employee provides administrative, event, and outreach support while helping create an inclusive and welcoming environment that respects the diverse faith traditions and beliefs represented within the campus community. ________________________________________ Responsibilities and Career Competencies Assist with planning, coordinating, and implementing campus worship services, chapel programs, spiritual formation activities, and interfaith events. C, TW, L Welcome students, faculty, staff, and community guests at Spiritual Life events while providing excellent customer service and hospitality. C, P, CTR Promote Spiritual Life programs through social media, digital communications, campus announcements, and printed promotional materials. C, T, CSD Provide administrative support by maintaining calendars, preparing event materials, organizing files, and responding to routine inquiries. P, T, C Assist with organizing community service projects, mission opportunities, and volunteer initiatives that strengthen campus and community partnerships. CTR, TW, L Help maintain the Spiritual Life office, chapel, and event spaces by preparing rooms, organizing supplies, and ensuring welcoming, functional environments. P, TW Maintain attendance records, volunteer databases, and program evaluations while ensuring confidentiality when appropriate. T, P, CT Collaborate with student organizations, campus departments, and community partners to encourage student engagement and participation in Spiritual Life programming. TW, C, CTR Participate in leadership development opportunities and serve as a positive role model by demonstrating integrity, respect, inclusion, and professionalism. L, P, CSD Assist with special projects, retreats, and campus-wide events as assigned by the Director of Spiritual Life or supervising staff. TW, L, CSD ________________________________________ Competencies/Skills Communication (C): Demonstrates effective written, verbal, and interpersonal communication while interacting with students, faculty, staff, and community members. Teamwork (TW): Collaborates effectively with supervisors, student leaders, volunteers, and campus partners to accomplish shared goals. Professionalism (P): Exhibits integrity, reliability, confidentiality, ethical conduct, and respect for individuals from diverse backgrounds and faith traditions. Career & Self Development (CSD): Develops leadership abilities, organizational skills, and professional work habits while exploring careers in ministry, nonprofit leadership, higher education, or public service. Critical Thinking (CT): Assists in organizing programs, solving logistical challenges, evaluating events, and identifying opportunities for continuous improvement. Community Transformation (CTR): Contributes to a campus culture of service, compassion, belonging, and civic engagement through outreach and community partnerships. Leadership (L): Demonstrates initiative, supports peer engagement, models servant leadership, and assists in leading campus programs and service opportunities. Technology (T): Utilizes Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, social media platforms, event management tools, and digital communication technologies to support office operations and student engagement. Qualifications: